{% set local_problem %}
<p>Hartselle's mix of established <a href="/recent-projects/">neighborhoods</a> and rural properties means diverse tree coverage — and diverse gutter challenges. Downtown homes are shaded by mature hardwoods dropping massive leaf loads every fall. Properties outside town often have pine coverage shedding needles year-round. And the agricultural areas surrounding Hartselle have their own debris patterns from crops and field edges.</p>

<p>For many of our Hartselle neighbors, gutter cleaning becomes a dreaded chore. Climbing ladders several times a year isn't just inconvenient — it's risky, especially for homes with steep roofs or two stories. Professional cleaning costs $150-200 per visit, and most homes need it 3-4 times annually. That's $600-800 a year for a chore that never ends.</p>

<p>The bigger concern is what happens between cleanings. When gutters clog during heavy storms, water overflows toward your foundation. Morgan County's clay-heavy soil doesn't absorb that water — it pools, saturates, and eventually finds its way into basements and crawlspaces.</p>

<p><strong>Our strong opinion:</strong> Don't buy foam gutter inserts from the hardware store. We've pulled thousands of feet of failed foam out of Hartselle gutters over the years. They decompose in Alabama's humidity, trap debris inside instead of keeping it out, and become part of the clog within 18-24 months. Spend on micro-mesh or don't bother at all.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Gutter guards are protective covers fitting over or inside your gutters. They let water through while blocking leaves, pine needles, twigs, and debris. Simple concept — but product selection matters enormously.</p>

<p><strong>What works in Hartselle:</strong> Micro-mesh guards with surgical-grade stainless steel screen. Holes around 50 microns — small enough to block even fine pine needles. Water sheets through; debris sits on top and blows or washes off.</p>

<p><strong>What doesn't work here:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Foam inserts — decompose in humidity, become part of the clog within 2 years (we've seen this hundreds of times)</li>
<li>Plastic snap-in screens — warp in Alabama heat, don't block fine debris</li>
<li>Brush-style guards — pine needles embed in bristles, nearly impossible to clean</li>
</ul>

<p>For Hartselle homes with mixed tree coverage — pine and hardwood together — micro-mesh handles everything. For homes with only large-leaf hardwoods and no pine, screen guards can work at lower cost.</p>

<p><strong>The realistic expectation:</strong> Quality guards reduce cleaning 90%+, not 100%. You might brush surface debris off once a year. That's dramatically better than scooping decomposed sludge four times a year, but it's not "never touch them again."</p>
{% endset %}

